  number={},
  pages={283-289},
  abstract={Glioblastoma Multiforme (GBM) is characterized by aggressive growth and high recurrence rates, making early and spatially precise identification of recurrence pathways critical for effective treatment planning and long-term disease management. Conventional MRI-based assessment relies heavily on manual interpretation and visual inspection, offering limited insight into tract-level tumour infiltration within the brain's white-matter architecture and providing little support for understanding preferential routes of tumour spread. This work presents a multimodal, tract-aware computational framework that integrates deep learning-based tumour localization with biomechanics-inspired tumour-tract interaction modelling. Using paired Fluid-Attenuated Inversion Recovery (FLAIR) and Colour Diffusion Tensor Imaging (DTI), the framework captures complementary information on tumour morphology and whitematter structural organization. A controlled synthetic augmentation strategy is employed to generate anatomically consistent occurrence-recurrence image pairs, enabling robust training of a YOLOv8 model used exclusively for automated tumour localization without manual region-of-interest annotation. Following detection, tumour boundaries are analysed in relation to surrounding white-matter tracts through a set of geometric, directional, and structural descriptors, including spatial proximity, directional alignment, boundary curvature, and local tumour density. These features are fused to model tumour-fibre softcontact interactions at a sub-tract level. Dominant Infiltration Vectors (DIVs) are derived to capture preferential directions of tumour spread, while recurrence likelihood is propagated across an anatomical tract graph to simulate connectivity-constrained infiltration dynamics. The proposed framework generates interpretable tract-restricted recurrence heatmaps and directional infiltration maps, providing clinically meaningful visualization of potential recurrence pathways. By linking visible tumour regions with their likely structural spread along white-matter tracts, this approach offers a transparent and extensible foundation for tractlevel recurrence analysis, supporting surgical planning, therapy targeting, and longitudinal disease monitoring.},
