  year={2024},
  volume={},
  number={},
  pages={174-178},
  abstract={Modern medical care generates large amounts of multimodal patient data, which many physicians find challenging to assess and synthesize into actionable knowledge. In the last few decades, AI has shown to be a helpful tool in this field. Hepatology is not an exception, as the application of AI techniques for the diagnosis and treatment of liver disease is the subject of an increasing amount of published research in the field. Artificial Intelligence (AI) has now enabled a faster access to fresh data on patients' chronic liver diseases, such as non-alcoholic fatty liver disease and liver fibrosis. This study determines the predictive capacity of machine learning, an application of artificial intelligence, for liver disease. The liver disease prediction (LDP) approach, which can be used by researchers, students, stakeholders, and health professionals to forecast liver illness, is introduced in this work. Random Forest, Linear Discriminant Analysis, Decision Tree, Classification and Regression Trees and BRDT are the five approaches that are selected. R and Python are used to assess the accuracy and determine which classification technique best predicts liver illness. The results showed that BRDT achieved the maximum accuracy of 93%, while the auto encoder network achieved an accuracy of 94.6%, which is more than acceptable and might be considered for the prediction of liver illness.},
  keywords={Accuracy;Machine learning algorithms;Liver diseases;Supervised learning;Prediction algorithms;Classification algorithms;Stakeholders;Security;Medical diagnostic imaging;Random forests;AI;algorithms;Data analysis;liver disease;prediction;machine learning},
