  abstract={Colorectal cancer metastasis prediction using SEER data is challenged by severe class imbalance, which limits the reliability of global metrics such as Accuracy and ROC-AUC. This study compared eight experimental strategies, including a no-balancing baseline, SMOTE variants, and Random OverSampling (ROS), across seven machine learning models using stratified 10-fold cross-validation. Models were evaluated with global and minority-oriented metrics, including Recall, F1-score, Balanced Accuracy, and AUC-PR. Performance varied according to the evaluation metric. ROS + KNN achieved the highest Recall (0.7423), while non-balanced models performed better on global metrics, with XGBoost reaching the highest Accuracy (0.8760) and LightGBM the highest AUC-PR (0.4774). SMOTE-based methods did not consistently outperform ROS or the no-balancing baseline across the evaluated metrics. Results highlight a trade-off between sensitivity and specificity and indicate that global metrics may overestimate performance in imbalanced scenarios. No universally superior balancing strategy was identified. Effectiveness depends on the evaluation metric and prediction objective, with minority-oriented metrics providing a more informative assessment of rare event detection.},
