  title={Small Intestine Cancer Prediction using Deep Learning: A Comparative Study of CNNs and RNNs}, 
  year={2024},
  volume={},
  number={},
  pages={1-5},
  abstract={Small bowel cancer is one of the rare tumors that often stays untreated until it has spread to a more advanced stage. Cancer is the leading cause of death in the globe. Early detection is critical for successful treatment, and machine learning algorithms have showed promise in detecting cancer at its early stages. In this article, we offer a deep learning-based technique to predicting small intestinal cancer that employs Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) and Recurrent Neural Networks (RNNs). We used a dataset of clinical characteristics and biomarkers from small intestinal cancer patients to train and evaluate our models. Our findings reveal that the proposed models were highly accurate in predicting small intestinal cancer, with the CNN-based model slightly outperforming the RNN-based model. We also compared our approach to other cutting-edge methodologies, and discovered that the proposed models surpassed them in terms of accuracy. Our study indicates the potential of deep learning-based approaches for predicting small intestine cancer and provides information on the relative efficacy of CNNs and RNNs. Our findings can help design more accurate and efficient cancer prediction models for small intestinal cancer.},
  keywords={Deep learning;Intestines;Recurrent neural networks;Biological system modeling;Predictive models;Convolutional neural networks;Cancer;BI analysis;formatting;Image Processing;Intestine Cancer;Machine Learning;Prediction Model;Normalization},
