  year={2025},
  volume={},
  number={},
  pages={1-8},
  abstract={Colorectal cancer (CRC) has become the second most deadly cancer globally, with many cases beginning as polyps. Early and precise detection of these polyps is crucial for effective CRC treatment. Currently, polyp segmentation largely relies on deep learning models such as U-Net and its variants. Although these models perform well in identifying lesions with clear boundaries, they often neglect the complex interactions between structural connections and pixel areas. This oversight can impair the detection and characterization of small and early-stage polyps, potentially leading to suboptimal treatment outcomes. To address this issue, this study introduces Polyp-GSAM, an algorithmically enhanced model designed for the precise delineation of polyps. This model extends the foundational principles of the Segment Anything model (SAM), incorporating a synthesis of multi-scale semantic attributes and fine-grained spatial particularities within the context of medical imaging. By integrating an Image Graph Convolutional Attention Encoder (IGCA) with a SAM hybrid framework, the IGCA module allows the model to effectively utilize the deep structural features of imaging data. Additionally, the CS-Adapter module which include Channel Adapter and Space Adapter finely tunes the SAM encoder to better suit polyp imaging. Our model innovatively applies graph attention mechanisms to capture the complexity of inter-pixel relationships and hierarchical Structures, overcoming limitations of previous models that failed to consider such complexities. Extensive evaluations on five benchmark datasets have demonstrated that Polyp-GSAM significantly surpasses existing state-of-the-art methods in polyp segmentation tasks, marking it as a powerful tool for early colorectal cancer intervention. Code is available at: https://github.com/Keno-XK/Polyp-GSAM.},
