  booktitle={2025 1st International Conference on Advancement in Futuristic Technologies (ICAFT)}, 
  title={SympCare: A Web-Driven Diagnostic Framework for CBC-Based Disease Prediction Using Machine Learning}, 
  year={2025},
  volume={},
  number={},
  pages={1-8},
  abstract={Disease prediction has become an essential component of modern healthcare, particularly in environments where timely clinical assessment is limited. This paper presents a detailed examination of intelligent health advisory systems that use machine learning to analyze clinical records, imaging data, and hematological parameters. The surveyed studies highlight the growing role of structured datasets, automated information extraction, and feature-driven models in improving early diagnosis across a wide range of conditions. While these systems have shown encouraging performance, many continue to depend on symptom reporting or multimodal data sources that are not always available or reliable in routine clinical practice.To address this gap, the paper introduces SympCare, an AI-based diagnostic system designed to predict diseases using only Complete Blood Count (CBC) values. SympCare removes dependence on subjective symptoms and uses a web-based interface linked to a Flask-driven backend for real-time analysis. The system preprocesses CBC inputs, extracts relevant features, and applies a trained machine learning model to generate immediate diagnostic predictions. By focusing on objective hematological indicators and a lightweight deployment framework, SympCare improves accessibility and consistency in early disease screening, particularly for regions where laboratory reports are available but medical consultation is delayed. The combined survey and system contribution offer a clear perspective on current developments while presenting a practical solution that strengthens the reliability of CBC-based disease diagnosis.},
