  year={2025},
  volume={},
  number={},
  pages={513-520},
  abstract={The rapid growth of clinical databases and electronic health records has facilitated the implementation of deep learning for predictive analytics in healthcare, particularly in high-risk and critical care settings. However, current models typically fall short in capturing long-term prognostic trajectories and short-term diagnostic signals simultaneously, reducing their usefulness at the bedside. To overcome this gap, we propose a Transformer-Based Multi-Task Learning (TBMT) architecture by integrating length-of-stay estimation and mortality prediction into a unified framework. The model uses task-specific output layers and self-attention mechanisms to capture high-level temporal relationships in patient data for both diagnosis-oriented and prognosis-oriented predictions. Our model outperforms baselines, including CNN, LSTM, Random Forest, and XGBoost, on the MIMIC-III dataset, which comprises over 61,000 ICU admissions, with a particular focus on sepsis patients. The TBMT model’s accuracy, precision, recall, and F1-score are 91.54%, 87.94%, 92.10%, and 89.98%, respectively. By contrast, the accuracy of XGBoost is 90.5%. Our results show that multi-task deep learning can improve robust decision-support systems in critical care by bridging the gap between diagnosis and prognosis},
  keywords={Feeds;Antennas;Radio broadcasting;Frequency modulation;MIMICs;Filtering;Filters;Millimeter wave integrated circuits;Monolithic integrated circuits;Gabor filters;Transformer-Based Multi-Task Learning (TBMT);CNN;LSTM;Random Forest;XGBoost},
