  pages={1-8},
  abstract={Lung Cancer is one of the major contributor to cancer-related deaths, caused by uncontrolled growth of abnormal cells that form tumors that are typically classified as Small Cell Lung Cancer (SCLC) or Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer (NSCLC). Early detection significantly improves the survival rates also crucial for effective treatment, yet relying on traditional methods that manually interprets CT scans or histopathological images are time consuming, subjective and difficult to scale for large clinical practices. This paper analyzes existing methodologies that utilizes Artificial Intelligence (AI) and Deep Learning (DL) models, especially Vision Transformers (ViTs) and Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) that automatically extracts complex and layered features from the imaging data directly which allows for more accurate and consistent diagnosis. Hybrid architectures such as CNN-ViT and combinations like LEViT strengthen feature representation by capturing both local and global patterns and attention mechanisms such as Squeeze-and-Excitation (SE) blocks and grid or attention block helps the model focusing on high cancer-relevant regions without excessive computational load. Moreover, Explainable AI (XAI) techniques that includes Grad-CAM and LIME makes the process of decision-making more transparent. Altogether, these approaches provide precise, efficient and applicable diagnostic tools that minimizes errors supporting earlier interventions. The outcome demonstrates the capability of ML and DL models analysed for lung cancer detection and classification.},
